"title": "What tantra is",
"html": "    <div class=\"home-emblem\"><svg class=\"il\"><use href=\"#il-lotus\"/></svg></div>\n    <p class=\"lead\">Tantra is not \"sex that lasts for hours\" and not a technique for a better orgasm, although both of those tend to happen along the way. It is an approach in which the body, the breath and touch are a way to be fully present, with yourself and with the person in front of you. Regular sex has a destination, and everything rushes toward it. Tantra has no destination, and so there is time.</p>\n\n    <h2>Where it comes from</h2>\n    <p>The word \"tantra\" in Sanskrit means weaving, or expansion. It refers to Indian and Buddhist traditions more than a thousand years old that saw the body, desire included, not as an obstacle to the spiritual but as a gateway to it. Most of the original tantra was not about sex at all: rituals, mantras, breath and meditation. What is known today in the West as \"tantra\" (neo-tantra) joins those principles with modern bodywork, massage and intimacy between partners. This app belongs to that stream: not a religion, not mysticism, but a set of practices that work on the body and on the bond.</p>\n\n    <h2>The terms that keep coming back here</h2>\n    <div class=\"grid2\">\n      <div class=\"term\"><b>Breath</b>The central tool. Deep breathing into the belly and pelvis spreads arousal from the genitals to the whole body, slows the way to the peak, and syncs the two of you. Almost every task here begins with it.</div>\n      <div class=\"term\"><b>Gaze</b>Eyes open, even in the strong moments. That is what turns the other's body from an object into a person, and sex from an act into a meeting.</div>\n      <div class=\"term\"><b>Yoni and lingam</b>The tantric words for the female and male genitals. \"Sacred place\" and \"pillar of light\". Not a polite nickname but a change of attitude: you touch them like a ritual, not like a button.</div>\n      <div class=\"term\"><b>Giver and receiver</b>Instead of \"leader and led\". The giver devotes all their attention to the other's body without expecting anything back in that moment. The receiver only receives. For most people, receiving without giving back is the hardest thing here.</div>\n      <div class=\"term\"><b>Energy</b>Call it prana, chi, or simply arousal. It is what you feel rising in the body when you breathe into touch. The practices here teach you to feel it, to move it from the pelvis to the heart and head, and not to spill it in one quick peak.</div>\n      <div class=\"term\"><b>Pelvic floor</b>The muscles that stop urine. Squeezing and releasing in the rhythm of the breath increases sensation, allows control at the edge, and in men is the basis for orgasm without ejaculation.</div>\n    </div>\n\n    <h2>The principles</h2>\n    <ul>\n      <li><b>Slow.</b> Everything you do in regular sex, twice as slow. Then twice as slow again. Slowing down is not a lack of desire, it is the way to feel every inch.</li>\n      <li><b>No destination.</b> Orgasm may happen and may not. When it is not the goal, the body stops rushing toward it, and arousal stays high for much longer.</li>\n      <li><b>All body.</b> The genitals are part of the body, not its center. In tantric massage they get the same time as the knee, and precisely because of that, when you reach them, it is different.</li>\n      <li><b>Voice.</b> A sigh, a hum, an \"ahhh\" on the exhale. Voice is breath made visible. The lower it is, the more arousal sinks into the pelvis and stays there longer.</li>\n      <li><b>Presence.</b> Phones out of the room, a candle, time. A small ritual at the start (a candle, an intention, a bow) tells the body that this evening is different.</li>\n    </ul>\n    <div class=\"pull\">The most intimate thing you can do with someone is breathe with them at the same pace, eyes open, in no hurry to get anywhere.</div>\n\n    <h2>The core practices</h2>\n    <ul>\n      <li><b>Gaze and shared breath</b>: three minutes of wordless eye contact, 4·6 breathing, mouth-to-mouth breathing. Sounds simple, and changes everything that follows.</li>\n      <li><b>Slow touch and massage</b>: feather touch, warm oil, \"yes / no / slower\". The body relearns where it enjoys when there is no goal.</li>\n      <li><b>Yoni and lingam massage</b>: genital massage as a ritual of twenty minutes or more, with breath, without a goal. Including the \"sacred spot\": the G-spot in women, the prostate in men. For many it is the first time the genitals receive long attention that is not on the way to something.</li>\n      <li><b>Build and stop</b> (tantric edging): reaching 7, 8, 9 out of 10 and stopping, breathing, moving the heat upward. After a few rounds the body can reach a \"valley orgasm\": long waves without a single peak, or a much deeper peak when it finally arrives.</li>\n      <li><b>Yab-yum and slow sex</b>: the seated embrace position (the receiver in the giver's lap), penetration without movement, movement only in the rhythm of the breath, a pause every minute. Sex turns from effort into shared breathing.</li>\n      <li><b>Worship</b>: kisses over the whole body, mouth and tongue as ritual, slow 69. The difference from regular oral sex is the pace and the intention: there is no \"finishing\", there is \"adoring\".</li>\n    </ul>\n\n    <h2>What happens in the body</h2>\n    <p>When you slow down and breathe deeply, the nervous system shifts from effort (sympathetic) to calm and receptivity (parasympathetic). Arousal inside calm is a state most people do not know from regular sex: the body is many times more sensitive, time stretches, and trembling, laughter or tears in the middle are a natural response to release, not a malfunction. Men learn to separate orgasm from ejaculation; women often discover arousal and orgasms from places they did not know about. And after such an evening there is usually no \"drop\", but a quiet closeness that lasts for days.</p>\n\n    <h2>How the evening is built here</h2>\n    <p>The deck warms up slowly: four tasks at depth 1 (breath, gaze, touch), four at depth 2 (massage, voice, positions), four at depth 3 (yoni and lingam, energy, union), and always \"Closing\" at the end. Most tasks have a giver and a receiver, and the \"switches every task\" mode (the default) swaps you each time, so that you both give and you both receive. \"Stop\" stops everything. \"Slow\" slows down and stays. Those two words are part of the ritual.</p>\n\n    <h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n    <ul>\n      <li><b>Rushing the breath.</b> When the body gets excited the breath shortens. Bringing it back to the belly is the whole practice.</li>\n      <li><b>Skipping the opening.</b> Without three minutes of gazing or a small ritual, the body stays in everyday mode.</li>\n      <li><b>Giving and receiving at the same time.</b> When one gives, the other only receives. Mutuality is in the whole evening, not in every moment.</li>\n      <li><b>Judging what comes up.</b> Laughter, tears, trembling, boredom, sudden arousal: all of it is fine. Talk about it in the morning.</li>\n    </ul>\n    <div class=\"pull\">There is no task here you can fail. Whoever breathed, looked and touched has done the task.</div>\n\n    <h2>Where the name comes from</h2>\n    <p>Breath: the first thing you do when you are born, the last when you die, and what most of the time in between you forget to do all the way. This evening starts from it.</p>\n"
